If your parents earned twice what a neighbor's parents earned, how much more will you earn
compared to the neighbor's children? This questions is the motivation for the Great Gatsby curve
and the "American Dream" debate. Policy economists comparing countries, people arguing about
whether the US is a land of opportunity. connect more to economics

Paragraph 2: the question, stated as a question. Then the twist this post is about: the answer depends on WHEN you measure
income — and measuring it at the wrong moment can hide almost half of the persistence. This post walks through the
whole pipeline, from raw survey file to estimation using SQL inside Python, so a reader with basic Python and SQL can redo it.]

## What the intergenerational elasticity measures

Intergenerational elasticity (IGE) measures the link between parents' income and 
their children's income after they leave the family. The equation is:

$$
\ln y_{\text{child}} = \alpha + \beta\, \ln y_{\text{parent}} + \varepsilon
$$
It is a log-log regression of child income on parent income, where β is the IGE. A
β of 0.4 means that if one father earned 10% more than another, his children earn 4% more
than the other's children on average. A β of 0 means there is no relationship, and a β of 1 would mean a caste system
where there is no mobility whatsoever. 

There are two problems 
(1) one year of income is a noisy reading of a family's long-run position (a bonus, a layoff, a farm loss);
(2) a 25-year-old's income is a poor reading of their lifetime income (the high-earners-to-be are still in grad school).]

## Introduction of the data source

The Panel Study of Income Dynamics (PSID) began in 1968 with about
5,000 US families and has interviewed the same people, their children, and their grandchildren. 
Because children are followed when they leave home, the same file contains a father's income in 1985
and his daughter's income in 2015. That is what makes a parent–child match possible.

We rely on two unique identifiers for the match:
- **Every person's permanent ID.** This is the interview number of the family they were first observed in 1968 (for the original
  families) plus a person number within it. It never changes.
- **Interview number each family gets every year.** Income is recorded once per family per year
  under that number, and each member's record carries the same number. So a person's record says "in 1990 I was in family 517",
  and the family record says "family 517 earned $38,400 in 1989".

The raw data downloaded from the Data Center gives one wide spreadsheet (85,536 people × 308 columns, one column per variable per
year), and the first job is to turn it into three tables. A table with individuals, a table with one row per
person-year and a table with one row per family-year can be joined on (year, interview number)

| Table | One row per | Rows | Key |
|---|---|---|---|
| `individuals` | person | 85,536 | permanent ID (`id_1968`, `person_num`) |
| `persons` | person × survey year | 910,869 | permanent ID + `year`; carries that year's `interview`, `age`, relationship to head |
| `families` | family × survey year | 320,456 | `year`, `fam_interview`; carries total family income (CPI-adjusted to 2023 dollars) |

## Step 1: Filter the children

A child observation is a person-year in which the person becomes the head of their own household
or is the spouse of the head, aged 25–45. The relationship filter is needed and 
we can't rely on age alone. For example, a 32-year-old still
living with their parents carries the parents' income on their record.

```sql
SELECT id_1968, person_num, year, age, interview
FROM persons
WHERE rel_head IN (1, 2, 10, 20, 22) #survey code change
  AND age BETWEEN 25 AND 45;
```

SELECT picks columns, FROM chooses the table, and WHERE filters those rows we need. Note that there is
code change in 1983, so 1 and 2 are before 1983 and 10, 20, and 22 are after. Refer to the codebook for more information. 
We should get 259,398 persons-years from 34,533 people.

## Step 2: Attach each person-year to its family's income

The JOIN clause lines up rows of two tables that share a key. The key here is (year, interview
number). We should check the row count before and after JOIN. Same count (259,398 → 259,398) means
nothing was duplicated and every person-year found its family.

```sql
SELECT p.id_1968, p.person_num, p.year, p.age, f.fam_income_real
FROM persons AS p
JOIN families AS f
  ON p.year = f.year AND p.interview = f.fam_interview
WHERE p.rel_head IN (1, 2, 10, 20, 22)
  AND p.age BETWEEN 25 AND 45;
```

We use INNER JOIN here because we want to drop a person-year with no family record.
That is, the family is not interviewed during that year. 

## Step 3: JOIN on identifiers to find each child's father

The `individuals` table has the child's own ID in two columns and the father's ID in two other columns.
Joining the table to itself — the child's father columns against the father's own ID columns — is the parent–child match.

```sql
SELECT c.id_1968, c.person_num,            
       dad.id_1968, dad.person_num         
FROM individuals AS c
LEFT JOIN individuals AS dad
  ON c.father_id_1968 = dad.id_1968
 AND c.father_person_num = dad.person_num;
```

A LEFT JOIN here keeps every child and fills the father columns with NULL when no match
exists, so the NULLs can be counted. Of 34,533 people in the child sample, 24,690 have no
father in the data. Among the 24,690 NULLs, 15,316 was because their parents were never 
surveyed, and 9,374 was because the father's ID points to someone not in the extract. 
The matched children we get are those whose fathers were PSID members. 

We then join the father's ID to his rows in `persons`, and those to `families`, keeping the years when
the child was 13–17. The reason we use a window between 13-17 is that it measures 
the family's resources while the child was growing up. 
It is the same ages for every child, and it falls in the annual-interview era for most of the sample.

```sql
SELECT c.id_1968, c.person_num,
       fp.year, fp.year - b.birth_year AS child_age,
       f.fam_income_real
FROM individuals AS c
JOIN birth    AS b  ON b.id_1968 = c.id_1968 AND b.person_num = c.person_num
JOIN persons  AS fp ON fp.id_1968 = c.father_id_1968
                   AND fp.person_num = c.father_person_num
JOIN families AS f  ON f.year = fp.year AND f.fam_interview = fp.interview
WHERE fp.year - b.birth_year BETWEEN 13 AND 17;
```

## Step 4: GROUP BY and AVG: one year of income versus several

After JOINing, we then aggregate the data to get the income information we want. 
GROUP BY collapses many rows per child into one, and we are grouping by two
identifiers, so both conditions need to be met. 
AVG takes the mean and COUNT gives the number of rows. The two parent
income measures: the single year when the child was closest to 15, and the average over the observed years at ages 13–17
(at least two years required, so both measures use the same 3,951 children — say why that matters: the gap between the two
estimates is then measurement, not sample.

```sql
SELECT id_1968, person_num,
       AVG(fam_income_real) AS parent_inc_avg,
       COUNT(*)             AS parent_years
FROM father_income
GROUP BY id_1968, person_num;
```

[WRITE — one sentence on the child side: the same GROUP BY gives the child's average family income over ages 30–40, and a
window function picks the single-year readings at ages 25, 30, 35, 40 and 45 for the next section.]

## Result: Lifecycle bias for a single year

![](fig1_ige_single_vs_multi.png)

[WRITE — explain the figure before interpreting it (Post 3 lesson): each dot is the average child income within a bin of
parent income; the lines are the regression fits; both axes are log scales so the slope IS β. Then the numbers from
`ige_estimates.csv`: single-year parent income gives β = 0.25; averaging the same fathers over 13–17 gives β = 0.43. Say what that
means in plain words: the one-year measure understates how much of a parent's advantage persists by about 40%. Relative framing
(Post 3 lesson): "relative to the single-year baseline, averaging raises the estimate from 0.25 to 0.43".]

[WRITE — why, in two sentences, no jargon: a single year's income = long-run income + that year's luck. The luck part is
noise in the regressor, and noise in a regressor pulls the slope toward zero (attenuation). Averaging cancels some of the luck,
so the slope recovers. Mention that this is the classic result (Solon 1992; Mazumder 2005 pushed averages further and found
β closer to 0.6).]

## Result 2 — the answer also depends on how old the child is

![](fig2_ige_by_child_age.png)

[WRITE — explain the figure: each point is a separate regression using the child's income in a single year at that age
(parent income is always the 13–17 average); the band is the 95% confidence interval; the dashed line is the 30–40 average
from Result 1. Numbers from `ige_by_child_age.csv`: 0.25 at 25, 0.35 at 30, 0.45 at 35, 0.52 at 40, 0.45 at 45.]

[WRITE — this is life-cycle bias, defined plainly: at 25, children of high-income parents are disproportionately still in
school or at the bottom of steep career ladders, so their current income understates their lifetime income more than it does
for others. Measured at 25 the IGE looks like 0.25; measured at 40 it is twice that. Haider and Solon (2006) showed current
income tracks lifetime income best in the early 30s to early 40s, which is why the main estimate averages 30–40. Then the
composition caveat from the notebook: different children at each age (younger cohorts aren't 45 yet).]

## Why averaging is the better measure of long-run status

[WRITE — pull the two results together in one short section, keeping the two biases distinct (they are often conflated):
- measurement noise in parent income → fixed by averaging years (Result 1)
- life-cycle bias in child income → fixed by measuring near mid-career, and averaging across those years (Result 2)
Both go the same direction (down), so a study that measures one year of income at a young age can halve the true IGE.
State what "long-run economic status" means and why a multi-year average is closer to it.]

## Caveats

[WRITE from the notebook's decisions cell, in reader language:
- Only children whose fathers were in the study can be matched — children born into PSID families. Not a random sample of US
  children; the SRC sample is used without weights (the SEO low-income oversample as a robustness check gives 0.33 and 0.50).
- Family income, not the father's earnings: it includes the mother's earnings and transfers. Standard in this literature but not
  the only choice.
- Child's age in the father's years is inferred from birth year = min(year − age), which can be off by one.
- Negative and zero incomes (business and farm losses) are dropped before taking logs.
- Correlation of generations, not a causal effect of parental income.]

## Takeaway

[WRITE — two or three sentences with numbers, answering the opening question. E.g. "In the PSID, about 0.43 of a father's
(log) income advantage persists into his children's family income at ages 30–40 — but measure parent income in a single year
and you would report 0.25; measure the child at 25 and you would report 0.25 again. Nearly half of what gets passed on is
invisible to a one-year snapshot." Bookend: read with the opening paragraph.]

## Data and code

| Source | What | Access |
|---|---|---|
| Panel Study of Income Dynamics (PSID), public-use data, 1968–2023 | Individual file: permanent person ID, sex, father's and mother's IDs, and per wave age, interview number, relationship to head, sequence number. Family files: family interview number and total family money income | PSID Data Center custom extract (job J366383), downloaded 17 October 2026; variable list and code-to-name crosswalk in the repository |
| U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, CPI-U all items (CPIAUCSL), via FRED | Annual-average CPI used to express all incomes in 2023 dollars (PSID income in survey year *t* refers to calendar year *t* − 1) | FRED CSV endpoint, accessed 23 October 2026 |

Panel Study of Income Dynamics, public use dataset. Produced and distributed by the Survey Research Center, Institute for Social Research, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI (2025).

Code, the SQL, and every number in this post: [github.com/1iiiiii/Intergenerational-Mobility](https://github.com/1iiiiii/Intergenerational-Mobility).
